Q&A on Double Ninth Festival | How to View US Non-Farm Payrolls Data Surpassing Expectations in January and Future Trends - Economic Observer – A Professional Financial News Website
Q: How does Red Autumn Investment view the large surprise in January non-farm payroll employment data in the US and its future trend?
A: On February 3, the U.S. Department of Labor released the non-farm payroll data for January 2023, showing an increase of 5.17 million in non-farm jobs, significantly higher than the market expectation of 1.89 million; the unemployment rate fell to 3.4%, its lowest level in nearly 50 years; and hourly wages grew at a year-over-year rate of 4.4% and at a month-over-month rate of 0.3%, slightly slowing.
From a total perspective, there were many one-off and technical factors affecting this data, such as returning faculty and staff on strike, adjustments to survey statistics, and updates to seasonal adjustment factors. There may be further corrections (referencing the January 2022 revision of non-farm jobs from 504,000 to 364,000). Non-farm employment may not be as strong as the data suggests.
Structurally, layoffs in high-tech enterprises continue but at a lower level; high-contact services such as leisure hotels, education, and healthcare have seen significant job growth, with data showing that there is still a labor shortage in the service sector.
With the rebound in services PMI, job openings and labor shortages data, and the strength of consumer credit data, we judge that services in the US have rapidly recovered after extreme weather and holidays.
We believe that, without recession, the tightness in the U.S. labor market’s supply and demand cannot improve.
Currently, the US labor market has a shortfall of over 5 million in supply, and labor participation rates are rising slowly, with especially the labor participation rate for those aged 25-54 already approaching pre-pandemic highs.
Furthermore, strong consumer demand, driven by robust excess savings and consumer credit among American residents, will further delay the impact of tightening policies on the job market.
Persistent tightness in the US labor market supply and demand will be the backdrop to the US economy in 2023.
With interest rates currently nearing hikes and inflation expectations bouncing around, we remain pessimistic about the sustained slowdown in wage growth.
Before this data release, expectations for the Fed's interest rate path in both the US stock and bond markets were overly optimistic.
With continued improvement in inflation data, the Fed is simultaneously slowing its rate-hike cadence while maintaining an uneven balance on market inflation expectations, but markets are ignoring the Fed’s hawkish rhetoric and are instead expecting early pause in hikes and two cuts this year-end.
With a tight labor market and uncertain impacts of contractionary policies, we consider the Federal Reserve’s guidance on interest rate hikes at year-end to be relatively reasonable. Adjustments in market extreme expectations and overcrowded trading may cause some harm to the market.
While wage growth stubbornly holds up against the current rapid decline in inflation, as M2 data turns negative and leading indicators of CPI improve gradually, US CPI has already shifted towards a phase of rapid decline since October. The stubbornness of wage growth will merely serve as a floor for future inflation data. Current market expectations are difficult to refute, and the market may enter a new round of games once the next inflation data is released.
A Quick Guide to Internet Finance
This book is quite superficial and could be considered an introductory course that helps people quickly get a grasp of the overall picture of internet finance, but for a deeper understanding, one still needs to follow the map and dig deeper on their own.
And given that the book was published earlier, the content about cultivating user habits through subsidies in later battles between Alipay and WeChat Pay has not been mentioned, it seems that the share of third-party payments today may not be as small as what was stated in the book.
Now, many people go out to eat with their phones, and merchants have also abandoned UnionPay due to high bank transaction volumes.
But I still want to thank the author for writing this introductory book, as it is clear that the author has at least several years of experience in financial observation and is well-versed in the history of both domestic and international finance, which allows them to produce such easily understandable content.
Writing grandiose things is easy, but writing in an understandable way is difficult.
