The book I read in Grade 10 was just done out of boredom, thinking it might not be something that would addict me since I had previously been less attracted to the web novels on Startings.
Then... I fell into a trap.
According to this plot, the entire book is quite light and pleasant. Even when Ye Xin was still in the online game, the rivalries between guilds were not heavy because they were often derailed by Ye Shen's dramatic twists and turns.
When it came to the competition part, there was a touch of melancholy about the changes and transitions of the times (which might just be me being sentimental).
Reading the whole book took me much of my high school time, but I just couldn't stop.
The depiction of full-time characters and emotional arcs is truly stunning, with some characters mentioned briefly but leaving a deep impression. Characters like Ye Xin, Huang Shaoting, and Su Mengchun have amassed numerous fans, while less central characters such as Jiaopaitao and Chu Yuxiu are also very likable. In an otherwise action-packed esports story, there is not even one character that can be despised to the extreme. This success lies in the way Full-Time portrays its characters.
And on the emotional front, part-time works do not have the common criticism of “thousand beauties and a hundred deformed” from general web novels, where everyone's emotions converge on their love for Honor this game and a desire for a fair victory.
Small measures indicate no great conspiracy; everyone seems so simple and adorable.
This might be why there are no unpleasant full-time roles.
Full-time IP is large, thanks to this.
Leaving aside this cultural phenomenon of web literature, being a full-time writer could be a decent book.
How much he really does provide some passion and a little bit of touch that moves people.
Full-time serialization will begin in April this year.
