Something like this was read long ago; I happened to jot down some reflections by memory.
This book is more of a collection of fragments about love from life, rather than a short story collection.
The tone of this book is extremely bland, like plain water that is bland, pursuing simplicity to an extreme in its choice of words and sentences, and the plot is also as quiet and bland as a street on a summer afternoon.
Every short story is a snapshot of a small moment in ordinary life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally, titled "What Do We Talk About When We Talk About Love?"
I believe that this collection of stories, along with its title, is a form of performance art. The author intends to convey that love is not just the joy and sorrow on stage, nor the eternal sea and broken stone in urban legends; it is not merely the entwined affection of lovers. Love is more than these, and it should not be confined to them either. Love also encompasses the mundane aspects of life—daily routines that can feel monotonous and unfulfilling—and the disorientation and avoidance of reality, as well as the uncertainty about the future.
Reading this book, you may recall couples arguing beside you, boring marriages of people you know, and unhappy relationships.
This is precisely why I would not recommend it for aesthetic reasons; both its prose and plot are utterly bland, unless your life is stormy and tumultuous or as sweet as living in a Candy Castle, in which case you might find reading it merely routine and boring.
However, on a deeper level of thought, the everyday plot does not impress or expand one's ideas and perspectives (which differs from García Márquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), and the brief segments do not explore any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of the romance.
What are the reasons for the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I wouldn't recommend reading this book; it doesn't really matter whether one reads it or not.
The author’s ideas are conveyed more through technique and genre than through the narrative plot, which is also a reason for the poor reading experience of this book.
